const numberKeys=['gapMm','distalMm','radiusMm','delayDays','durationDays','elongationMmDay','growthLagDays','alignment','schwannSupport','vascularSupport','scar','supportHalfLifeDays','intrinsicHalfLifeDays','targetHalfLifeDays','remyelinationLagDays','remyelinationDays','motorFraction','axonCount','dtDays','fieldNodes','seed'].filter(k=>PARAMETER_META[k]);
---
<Layout pageTitle="Nerve Regeneration: Model & Evidence" description="Equations, assumptions, primary research, species-specific evidence and numerical verification for the Peripheral Nerve Regeneration Laboratory." wide={true} compactHeader={true}>
<article class="nr-lab nr-methods">
 <span class="nr-eyebrow">Peripheral nerve regeneration / Model {MODEL_VERSION}</span><h1>Model, evidence &amp; numerical checks</h1>
 <p class="nr-intro">A transparent computational experiment in how nerve repair unfolds over space and time. Representative axon trajectories interact with a changing tissue environment, while structural arrival, pathway identity and maturation remain distinct.</p>
 <nav aria-label="Methods sections"><a href="/simulations/nerve-regeneration/">← Open the laboratory</a><a href="#scope">Model scope</a><a href="#equations">Equations</a><a href="#parameters">Parameters</a><a href="#verification">Numerical checks</a><a href="#sources">Primary research</a></nav>
 <div class="nr-model-note"><span class="nr-badge">Exploratory / uncalibrated</span><p>The equations form an original reduced model informed by the studies below. They do not reproduce a published regeneration dataset and have not been validated for clinical, animal-treatment or device-efficacy predictions.</p></div>
 <section id="scope"><h2>1. What the model represents</h2><div class="nr-method-grid"><div><h3>Local biological environment</h3><p>The disconnected distal axon population loses integrity, becomes debris and is cleared. Aggregate macrophage activity and a Schwann repair-state variable interact with debris removal. The bridge gains prescribed vascular and Schwann support from its ends. Fibrosis supplies an adjustable obstruction term.</p><p>These normalized states preserve biological distinctions and causal hypotheses. They are not measurements of cell numbers, oxygen, cytokines, c-Jun expression or collagen concentration.</p></div><div><h3>Individual axon trajectories</h3><p>Each representative parent axon receives seeded traits for initiation, potential speed, caliber and pathway geometry. Its tip advances along a synthetic curve at a rate set by the local environment. Tortuosity converts arc-length growth into axial advance. The same seed and parameters reproduce the same experiment.</p><p>Paths are generated from geometry and alignment assumptions before integration. Emergent growth-cone steering, chemotaxis, cell adhesion forces, collateral sprouting and molecular target recognition are not solved.</p></div></div>
 <h3>Keep the endpoints separate</h3><div class="nr-table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Readout</th><th>Exact interpretation</th><th>What it cannot establish</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><td>Distal entry</td><td>A parent axon has crossed the distal repair boundary.</td><td>Contact with an appropriate end organ.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Target-distance arrival</td><td>The tip has reached the axial target coordinate: gap plus specified distal distance.</td><td>A functional neuromuscular junction or sensory ending.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Fascicle-matched arrival</td><td>The arrived axon’s destination fascicle index matches its original synthetic fascicle.</td><td>Recovery of its original motor unit, receptor, organ or motor/sensory branch.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Myelin maturity</td><td>A local time-since-arrival exponential; the headline mean is evaluated at distal entry among entered parents.</td><td>Measured sheath thickness, internodal length, g-ratio or conduction velocity.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Conduction surrogate</td><td>Target-arrival maturation multiplied by a seeded caliber factor, averaged over all original parents.</td><td>Action potentials, CMAP, force, sensation or recovery probability.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Target receptivity</td><td>An independent adjustable denervation-age diagnostic.</td><td>Target survival, a clinical deadline or a contact-rescue model. It does not gate growth.</td></tr>
 </tbody></table></div><p>Motor and sensory labels identify representative fibers without encoding target-specific guidance. Noninitiating and stalled trajectories stay in the denominator and are not described as dead neurons.</p></section>
 <section><h2>2. Injury and repair architectures</h2><div class="nr-table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Scenario</th><th>Implemented distinction</th><th>Interpretation</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><td>Crush / preserved tracks</td><td>No gap or imposed repair delay; preserved fascicle alignment; distal degeneration and regrowth still occur.</td><td>Idealized preserved-pathway axonotmesis, not every crush severity.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Direct apposition</td><td>Cut ends touch with zero gap; alignment remains adjustable.</td><td>No surgical tension, suturing trauma or fascicular coaptation calculation.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Hollow conduit</td><td>No initial internal Schwann scaffold; prescribed ingrowth fronts populate the gap.</td><td>A generic architecture, without a commercial material or universal critical gap length.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Structured bridge</td><td>Internal support after placement, at an assumed architectural support fraction.</td><td>Generic supportive scaffold; not a fitted autograft, allograft, cellular or biohybrid treatment effect.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Delayed repair</td><td>Axotomy competence, recipient distal support and target clocks run from injury. The fresh bridge appears at placement and has its own support age.</td><td>Compare the same architecture at different delays with a common seed.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Unrepaired transection</td><td>Local sprouting without credited distal entry.</td><td>No spontaneous gap closure or neuroma morphology is resolved.</td></tr>
 </tbody></table></div><p>Presets combine geometry with example coefficients. Comparing them does not estimate treatment efficacy. Pin a run and vary one parameter when investigating a specific causal assumption.</p></section>
 <section id="equations"><h2>3. Equations and units</h2><p>Time is days since injury. Axial and transverse coordinates are millimeters. Environmental states and maturation indices are dimensionless. Numerical coefficients below are explicit assumptions of this release.</p>
 {MODEL_EQUATIONS.map(e=><section id={`equation-${e.id}`}><h3>{e.title}</h3><div class="nr-equation">{e.formula}</div><p>{e.description}</p><p class="nr-help"><span class="nr-source-label">Assumption boundary</span>{e.assumption}</p></section>)}
 <h3>Space, events and time integration</h3><p>Heun’s explicit predictor–corrector method integrates local reactions and axon advance. The internal maximum step is 0.25 day; the interface also offers 0.1 and 0.05 day refinement. Field nodes concentrate in the gap and near the distal stump, preserving the gap boundary when the target is far away. Spatial averages account for nonuniform cell widths.</p><p>Daily output is supplemented by initiation, distal-entry and target-arrival event times. Arbitrary replay-time population counts use individual events, so an interpolated graph does not become a fractional count of neurons. Trajectory storage is distinct from the solver step; its local simplification tolerance is not a guarantee of global geometric accuracy.</p><p>Schwann support describes still-denervated pathways. Reinnervating one representative trajectory does not reset the entire local environment; per-tube reinnervation feedback is absent. Local myelination has its own arrival clock and remains possible when repair-state support has declined.</p>
 <h3>Relationship to published computational models</h3><p>Podhajsky and Myers modeled interacting tissue populations using diffusion-reaction equations. Laranjeira and colleagues used a random-walk model of neurite growth in engineered constructs. Those studies establish computational precedents; the present local reactions, prescribed fronts and seeded curves do not reproduce either framework or inherit their calibration. <a href="#source-podhajsky-1995">Podhajsky–Myers</a> · <a href="#source-laranjeira-2022">Laranjeira et al.</a></p></section>
 <section id="parameters"><h2>4. Parameters and evidence class</h2><p>Human-scale and rat-scale choices identify context. Changing the species label does not silently rescale coefficients. Human and rodent evidence stays separately identified; neither setting is a validated species fit.</p><div class="nr-table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Initial value</th><th>Class</th><th>Interpretation</th></tr></thead><tbody>{numberKeys.map(key=><tr><td>{PARAMETER_META[key].label}</td><td>{DEFAULT_CONFIG[key]} {PARAMETER_META[key].unit}</td><td>{PARAMETER_META[key].sourceClass}</td><td>{PARAMETER_META[key].note}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
 <h3>Why each mechanism is present</h3>{PARAMETER_EVIDENCE.map(p=><section><h3>{p.label}</h3><p><span class="nr-source-label">{p.classification}</span>{p.evidence}</p><p>{p.implementation} {p.references.map(id=><a href={`#source-${id}`}>[{REFERENCES.findIndex(r=>r.id===id)+1}] </a>)}</p></section>)}</section>
 <section id="verification"><h2>5. Numerical verification and reproducibility</h2><p>Verification asks whether the implemented equations behave correctly. Biological validation additionally requires fitting and testing against independent, endpoint-specific experiments. That validation has not been performed.</p><p><strong>Recorded verification:</strong> {verification.tests.count} named numerical tests passed for engine <code>{verification.engine.sha256.slice(0,12)}</code>. Recorded environment: {verification.environment.node} on {verification.environment.platform}. These measurements describe numerical consistency, not agreement with biological outcomes.</p>
 <div class="nr-table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>Recorded result</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><td>Ideal constant-speed position</td><td>{verification.reference.maximumPositionErrorMm.toExponential(2)} mm maximum error</td><td>Closed-form solution for the test-only uniform ideal limit.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>0.25 versus 0.05 day integration</td><td>{verification.timestep.rows[0].maximumFrontErrorMm.toFixed(4)} mm maximum endpoint difference</td><td>Same seeded default experiment, finer integration.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Shared target-arrival events</td><td>{verification.timestep.rows[0].maximumSharedArrivalErrorDays.toFixed(4)} day maximum difference</td><td>Parents arriving in both integration runs.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Large numerical cohort</td><td>{(verification.benchmark.solveMs/1000).toFixed(2)} s for 512 parents over 730 days</td><td>One Node.js run at 0.1 day steps and a 500 mm distal path. Browser runtimes vary.</td></tr>
 </tbody></table></div>
 <details><summary>Recorded timestep-refinement endpoints</summary><div class="nr-table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Step / days</th><th>Distal entry</th><th>Target arrival</th><th>Fascicle match</th><th>Maximum position difference / mm</th></tr></thead><tbody>{verification.timestep.rows.map(row=><tr><td>{row.dtDays}</td><td>{row.distalEntry}</td><td>{row.arrived}</td><td>{row.fascicleMatched}</td><td>{row.maximumFrontErrorMm.toFixed(5)}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div></details>
 <div class="nr-check-grid"><div><h3>Exact limiting case</h3><p>Uniform ideal-reference tests compare x = v(t − lag), entry and target-arrival times with closed-form values.</p></div><div><h3>Conservation &amp; bounds</h3><p>Disconnected intact material transfers into debris and cleared material. State bounds, nonnegative advance and parent bookkeeping are checked.</p></div><div><h3>Event correctness</h3><p>No target arrival precedes entry, and no growth precedes initiation. Zero speed and unrepaired injuries cannot receive false arrival credit.</p></div><div><h3>Refinement</h3><p>Timestep and spatial-grid refinements test consistency. Small gaps remain resolved when the target is hundreds of millimeters away.</p></div><div><h3>Independent clocks</h3><p>Repair, denervation history, initiation, local arrival, myelin maturation and target receptivity remain distinct.</p></div><div><h3>Seeded replay</h3><p>Random traits are sampled before integration. Timestep changes do not redraw the cohort.</p></div></div>
 <h3>Exports and comparisons</h3><p>JSON contains the model version, normalized configuration, seed, full trajectories, daily measurements, fields, assumptions and diagnostics. CSV contains the daily aggregate series. Imports require an exact version match, preserve the supported configuration and recompute locally. Saved result objects are ignored; imported comparisons, ensembles and display state are not restored.</p><p>Repeated runs vary the random seed at fixed assumptions. The 10th–90th percentile band describes a finite stochastic ensemble. It is not patient variability, measurement uncertainty, coefficient uncertainty or a biological confidence interval. Sensitivity sweeps hold the seed fixed and vary one parameter across five values; the resulting differences are conditional on the chosen equations and the fixed observation horizon.</p></section>
 <section><h2>6. Anatomical display and unresolved biology</h2><p>The nerve is procedural: an idealized envelope with three fascicles, representative axons, Schwann spindles, vascular guides and myelin sleeves. This is neither electron-microscopy reconstruction nor patient anatomy. The model’s millimeter coordinates determine tip positions. Transverse scale and fiber diameter are enlarged with explicit labels and can be returned to unity.</p><p>The 3D view uses an injury window; the full-path plot includes the entire distal distance. Larger numerical cohorts keep every parent in analysis while the renderer uses a representative subset. Selecting any parent makes it inspectable. A labeled Canvas2D fallback uses the same paths, fields, event clocks and local myelin on devices without WebGL.</p><ul>{MODEL_LIMITATIONS.map(text=><li>{text}</li>)}</ul><p>Predictive validation would require selecting a species, nerve, injury, repair and endpoint; obtaining raw longitudinal observations; estimating identifiable coefficients; and testing held-out data with parameter uncertainty. Additional visual detail alone does not establish that validation.</p></section>
 <section id="sources"><h2>7. Primary-source evidence dossier</h2><p>Each original study is identified by biological context, its contribution to this model, and the boundary on its use. Interventions described in these papers are not automatically implemented in the simulator.</p>
 {REFERENCES.map((r,index)=><section class="nr-source" id={`source-${r.id}`}><div class="nr-source-index">[{String(index+1).padStart(2,'0')}]<br/>{r.year}</div><div><h3><a href={r.url} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">{r.title}</a></h3><p class="nr-source-meta">{r.authors} · {r.journal}<br/>{r.species}<br/>DOI: <a href={`https://doi.org/${r.doi}`} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">{r.doi}</a></p><p><span class="nr-source-label">Reported finding</span>{r.finding}</p><p><span class="nr-source-label">Use in this model</span>{r.modelUse}</p><p><span class="nr-source-label">Boundary</span>{r.limits}</p></div></section>)}</section>
